body:
A smooth \(n\)-dimensional manifold is a space whose points have neighbourhoods described by \(n\) real coordinates. These descriptions fit together through smooth, smoothly invertible changes of coordinates on overlaps. Standard definitions also impose separation and countability conditions so the space behaves well.
The terrain is two-dimensional because a ground point needs two independent local labels. An embedding picture may use height as a third number, but that does not add a third direction of motion within the surface.
\explanation{local}{Does locally Euclidean mean physically flat?}{It means a neighbourhood has the same smooth coordinate structure as an open region of \(\mathbb R^n\). It does not say that distances measured across that neighbourhood obey Euclidean geometry. Distances, angles and curvature require more structure, such as a metric. A rigid board provides labels by projection; it need not reproduce the ground's distances.}
\explanation{global}{Why not use one grid everywhere?}{A particular projection can cease to be one-to-one or smooth over a larger region. Some manifolds, such as a sphere, cannot be covered by one chart at all. Other regions do admit a single chart, even if curved. Curvature alone therefore does not prohibit global coordinates; the scientists' overlapping boards are a convenient local construction.}

The complete ground surface is the manifold \(M\). Temperature \(T(p)\) and a curvature estimate \(K(p)\) attach scalar readings to each point \(p\); changing labels does not change either reading. Grass growth assigns a tangent direction and magnitude at each point instead of a scalar.
\explain{survey}{math.m01_manifolds_and_coordinates.terrain_setup}{The terrain survey}{Meet the scientists and their boards}
\explain{board}{math.coordinate_chart.terrain_survey}{The board's coordinate chart}{How does one board label curved ground?}
The collection of overlapping descriptions lets the group discuss one surface without identifying it with a single flat map.

Here \(U\) is an open region of the manifold and \(x(U)\) is open in \(\mathbb R^n\). The chart assigns a unique coordinate tuple to each point in its region; its inverse identifies the point from those labels. Smoothness is understood with respect to the manifold's compatible charts.
\explain{manifold}{math.manifold.definition}{A smooth manifold}{What structure makes these labels possible?}
An atlas is a collection of compatible charts covering the manifold. On an overlap a point may have several coordinate tuples, but each refers to that same point.
\explanation{inverse}{Why require an inverse?}{If two ground points project to the same board position, the labels cannot tell them apart. If an inverse breaks down, calculations in those labels may cease to describe the surface smoothly. Shrinking or replacing the patch avoids that coordinate failure.}

Let \(x\) and \(y\) be overlapping charts. For a point \(p\) in their common region, the transition map takes \(x(p)\) to \(y(p)\). It is a smooth map between open subsets of coordinate space, with a smooth inverse.
\explain{charts}{math.coordinate_chart.definition}{Coordinate charts}{What are the two descriptions being related?}
The notation \(y^i=y^i(x^1,\ldots,x^n)\) abbreviates this composition; the inverse gives \(x^i=x^i(y)\). A relabelling moves no physical point and changes no measured scalar value.
\explanation{scalar}{Does the temperature formula stay the same?}{The reading stays the same, but its formula usually changes. Write \(T_x=T\circ x^{-1}\) and \(T_y=T\circ y^{-1}\). Then
\[T_y(y(p))=T(p)=T_x(x(p)).\]
For example, replacing a metre label by a centimetre label changes the numerical argument of the formula. This is why an invariant measurement need not have an unchanged coordinate expression.}

A peg in an overlap has coordinates \(x^1,x^2\) for one scientist and \(y^1,y^2\) for the other. The agreed rule \(y=y(x)\) identifies that same peg in both records.
\explain{board}{math.coordinate_chart.terrain_survey}{The board's coordinate chart}{How were the board coordinates established?}
Straight grids on the individual boards do not force their transition rule to be linear. Different tangent planes and projections of curved ground can give nonlinear conversions. Scales, orientations and obliqueness add further differences.
\explanation{affine}{When is a simple linear rule enough?}{Two straight grids describing one flat plane are related by an affine rule \(y^i=A^i{}_j x^j+b^i\), with invertible constant matrix \(A\). The constants \(b^i\) shift the origin. This special case is useful, but should not be assumed for neighbouring projections of curved terrain.}

The parameter \(\lambda\) labels successive events along the history of a particle or signal. For a massive clock it is often convenient to use \cref{Proper time}{sr.proper_time} \(\tau\); a light ray has no proper-time clock parameter of this kind.
\explain{manifold}{math.manifold.definition}{A smooth manifold}{What space contains this curve?}
\explanation{parameter}{Is the parameter another spacetime coordinate?}{No. It labels positions along this particular curve. The spacetime point \(\gamma(\lambda)\) already has its coordinate tuple. Two smooth monotonic parameter choices can describe the same oriented path while assigning different rates to its traversal. Physical time or proper time, when supplied, gives the rate a particular meaning.}

A curve \(\gamma\) specifies points of the manifold. A chart displays them as the coordinate functions \(x\circ\gamma\). Another chart gives \(y\circ\gamma\), usually with a different-looking graph.
\explain{charts}{math.coordinate_chart.definition}{Coordinate charts}{How do points acquire their plotted coordinates?}
\explain{transition}{math.coordinate_transformation.definition}{Changing coordinate labels}{How are the two plots related?}
A \cref{Minkowski diagram}{sr.minkowski_diagram} is one useful spacetime display; the drawing is not the worldline itself. Merely choosing a more complicated coordinate plot does not make a freely moving particle accelerate physically.

The mobile probe follows a smooth route \(\gamma:t\mapsto\gamma(t)\in M\), with \(t\) in seconds. In one board chart its track is \((x^1(t),x^2(t))\). Across an overlap another scientist records \((y^1(t),y^2(t))\). The formulas change, but the route does not jump.
\explain{transition}{math.coordinate_transformation.definition}{Changing coordinate labels}{How do neighbours describe the same crossing?}
\explanation{history}{Is the surface route itself a worldline?}{Strictly this is a curve on the spatial terrain. Including time produces the spacetime history \(t\mapsto(t,\gamma(t))\). A stationary probe has a constant surface position but still traces a history through time. The distinction keeps a two-dimensional ground map separate from spacetime.}

At each smooth point a worldline supplies an instantaneous direction. Its scale depends on the curve parameter. For a massive particle parametrised by proper time, this becomes its \cref{Velocity four-vector}{sr.velocity_four_vector}.
\explain{tangent}{math.tangent_space.curves}{From curves to tangent vectors}{How does a curve supply an instantaneous vector?}
The tangent lies in the tangent space at the event being crossed. It is not a finite arrow joining two separated events.

Choose a smooth curve with \(\gamma(0)=p\). Its derivative at the crossing is a tangent vector. Different curves may share this vector even if they bend differently after leaving \(p\).
\explain{route}{math.worldline.terrain_survey}{The mobile probe's surface route}{What curve are the scientists measuring?}
In coordinates the components are \(v^i=dx^i/dt|_0\). The parameter sets their scale; a change of coordinate labels changes the components, not the underlying vector.
\explain{jacobian}{math.coordinate_transformation.jacobian}{The Jacobian of a coordinate change}{How do the components change between boards?}
\explanation{equivalence}{Which curves have the same tangent?}{In any chart through \(p\), compare their coordinate derivatives using parameters with the same intended scale. If those derivatives agree at the crossing, the curves have the same tangent there. The invertible Jacobian ensures that agreement in one chart implies agreement in another.}

A tensor field assigns a tensor of a fixed type smoothly to each point in a region. A tensor at one point is a geometric object, not merely a table of numbers. Its components describe it in a chosen basis; changing the basis changes those components in a prescribed way.
\explain{vectors}{math.tangent_space.definition}{Tangent vectors at a point}{Recall the space of possible vector inputs}
\explain{covectors}{math.cotangent_space.definition}{Covectors at a point}{Recall a linear readout of a vector}
A scalar field assigns one number, a vector field assigns one tangent vector, and a covector field assigns one linear readout at each point. Higher-order tensors extend these constructions to several slots.

A component array describes a tensor only together with its basis and transformation behaviour. Two charts can assign different arrays to the same field if those arrays obey the tensor transformation law throughout their overlap. Equal array sizes or constant entries do not establish that relation.
\explain{labels}{math.coordinate_transformation.definition}{Changing coordinate labels}{What changes when the chart changes?}
The transformation law will make this precise. Not every array of quantities with index labels transforms tensorially; the connection coefficients used later in GR are an important warning.\explain{rule}{math.tensor_transformation_law.definition}{The tensor transformation law}{How should the component arrays be related?}

In a chosen coordinate basis, \(V^i\) lists a vector's components and \(\omega_i\) lists a covector's components. The superscript is an index, not a power: \(V^2\) means the second component here, whereas \((V^1)^2\) squares the first component.
An expression such as \(A^i{}_{j}\) has two index slots. Fixing both labels selects one entry of its component matrix.
\explain{type}{math.tensor_field.rank}{Tensor type and order}{What do the index positions tell us?}
\explanation{abstract}{What changes in abstract-index notation?}{Abstract indices such as \(a,b\) describe the tensor's slots and contractions without choosing a numerical basis. Thus \(A^a{}_{b}\) describes a type-\((1,1)\) tensor. Component indices describe entries after a basis is chosen. Either notation can have free or dummy indices; the distinction is not curved versus flat space.}
The calculations on this page use component indices unless explicitly stated otherwise.

On the two-dimensional terrain use \(i,j=1,2\). In four-dimensional spacetime this atlas usually uses Greek indices \(\mu,\nu=0,1,2,3\). Always establish the range before expanding a sum.
A four-vector therefore has four components, while a two-index spacetime array has 16 entries before symmetries. The number of values of an index records dimension; the number and positions of indices record tensor type.
\explanation{power}{A component label versus a power}{In two dimensions, \(V^i=(3,4)\) means \(V^1=3\) and \(V^2=4\). The square of the second component is \((V^2)^2=16\). Superscripts can serve different roles, so parentheses matter.}

A label repeated once upstairs and once downstairs in one term is summed over its range. In two dimensions,
\[A^i B_i=A^1B_1+A^2B_2.\]
For \(A^i=(2,-1)\) and \(B_i=(3,4)\), this is \(2(3)+(-1)(4)=2\). The repeated \(i\) is a dummy label: replacing it consistently by \(j\) changes nothing.
\explanation{free}{What is a free index?}{A free index is not summed; it appears once in each term. The equation \(C^i=A^i+B^i\) represents one equation for every allowed \(i\). In two dimensions it means \(C^1=A^1+B^1\) and \(C^2=A^2+B^2\). Every term on both sides must have matching free labels and index positions.}
\explanation{check}{Spot an invalid equation}{In \(T^{\mu\nu}=V^\mu\), the free index \(\nu\) appears only on the left, so the equation is incomplete. In contrast \(T^{\mu\nu}=V^\mu W^\nu\) has matching free indices. A dummy index should occur exactly twice in a term, once up and once down. Reusing it three times does not define an Einstein sum.}
\explain{pairing}{math.cotangent_space.basis}{The covector/vector pairing}{Why does an upper/lower contraction have geometric meaning?}

Write the two charts as \(x\) and \(y\). Define the Jacobian and its inverse by
\[\begin{aligned}
J^a{}_i&\coloneqq\frac{\partial y^a}{\partial x^i},\\
K^i{}_a&\coloneqq\frac{\partial x^i}{\partial y^a}.
\end{aligned}\]
All quantities are evaluated at the same point in the overlap. An upper index receives a \(J\) factor, while a lower index receives a \(K\) factor. A nonlinear chart change makes these matrices position-dependent; the rule still holds point by point.
\explain{jacobian}{math.coordinate_transformation.jacobian}{The Jacobian and inverse transition}{Where do these matrices come from?}
\explain{first-cases}{math.tensor_transformation_law.vector_covector}{Vector and covector transformations}{Why do upper and lower indices use opposite factors?}

Count the upper and lower indices in a component expression. A tensor of type \((r,s)\) has \(r\) upper and \(s\) lower indices; its total order, also called total rank here, is \(r+s\).
A scalar has type \((0,0)\) and order zero. A vector \(V^\mu\) has type \((1,0)\); a covector \(\omega_\mu\) has type \((0,1)\). Both have order one, but they transform differently. A metric \(g_{\mu
u}\) has type \((0,2)\) and order two. A mixed tensor \(A^\mu{}_{
u}\) has type \((1,1)\) and order two.
\explanation{count}{How many component entries are there?}{In \(n\) dimensions each index takes \(n\) values. An order-\(k\) tensor therefore has \(n^k\) entries before symmetries or constraints are used. A four-dimensional metric has \(4^2=16\) entries. Symmetry \(g_{\mu
u}=g_{
u\mu}\) leaves 4 diagonal and 6 off-diagonal values, hence 10 independent components.}
\explanation{matrix-rank}{Is this the rank of a matrix?}{No. Matrix rank counts independent rows or columns of a linear map's array. Tensor order counts index slots. A zero matrix still represents an order-two tensor; its matrix rank is zero. Also, a tensor of order four can live on a two-dimensional manifold: order is not the dimension of the space.}
